I finished the edit stage for Tom Rhys Harries and wrote the three output files. Nothing was published, and no input files or live drafts were touched. All three files pass the pipeline's own validation: the evidence and resolution files match their schemas and the machine checks find no blockers. All 45 reviewer findings got a decision and are marked fixed. All 20 passages reviewers asked to protect are accounted for: 16 kept word for word and 4 reworded where they overstated the evidence.

**What changed in the article:**
- **The type call stays Type 6 at medium confidence, but the argument for it is now honest about how narrow it is.** Several reviewers showed that two of the draft's main reasons didn't hold up:
  - "For fear of losing this job" (his explanation for hiding the knee injury) fits Type 3 just as well, so it no longer decides between them.
  - His escapes during the screen tests (video games, bike rides) are typical Seven behavior, so they no longer count against Seven.
  - The case now rests on a few things he said himself. He called preparation a crutch and going in without it "terrifying." He finds relief in motorbikes because there's "nothing subjective about whether an engine works." He keeps crediting trusted collaborators, in 2020 and again in 2026. On release day he said he'd "gone past the point of nervousness."
- **Nine is now discussed.** A new section covers the calm side of the record that the draft left out: interviewers calling him "relaxed" and "clear-eyed and methodical," and his "very thick skin" line. It explains why Six still stays ahead.
- **Dating error fixed.** The draft said he'd described fear as his driver "since 2015." The 2015 source is him remembering beating himself up over missed auditions at drama school, with no mention of fear. The type answer, FAQ and ending now say "since at least 2022."
- **Fairness fixes.**
  - I added the director James Watkins's on-record answer that knowing about the knee "wouldn't have made any difference to me." I reopened that page and confirmed the quote before using it.
  - The ADHD diagnosis is now attributed to the magazine's reporting rather than to him, because none of his quoted words name the condition.
  - The Parade's End story now includes that Benedict Cumberbatch had the same concern before checking the book.
  - The tooth joke is no longer described as "self-mutilation."
- **Lasting accuracy.** The "sharpest test" framing of one post-release interview is now just a dated checkpoint, with the full quote restored, including "it might open a few more doors." Things that will change (no long-term colleague has spoken yet, what would change the read, his unnamed recent indie film) now carry dated reminders to recheck.
- **Headline and metadata.** The title no longer claims fear is "his engine," because those were never his words. The description says Type 6 "narrowly leads," with 7, 3 and 9 close.

**Two things you might want to veto:**
- **Length:** the body is now 3,827 words, up from 3,352. That's under the hard 4,500 limit but above the roughly 3,500 target. The jury asked for the Nine section, his home life and Watkins's reply; I made up for most of it by cutting repeated quotes from the middle sections.
- **ADHD FAQ:** one reviewer suggested deleting it. I kept it and reworded it to credit the magazine's 2022 report, which still meets that reviewer's bar. Delete it if you'd rather not have a health question in search results.

